Zero-dependency static generator — `src/content.js` is the single source of truth, `build.js` renders to `public/`. No frameworks, no `npm install`, nothing to audit at build time.

- **About Me** (`index.html`) — About, At-a-glance card, Research Interests, Education timeline, Selected Projects, Teaching & Mentorship, Skills & Tools, Contact
- **Blog** (`blog.html` + three full post pages) — grouped by year, with written bodies rather than the CV's `#` placeholder links
- **Publications** (`publications.html`) — all four papers, grouped by year, with venue, volume and topic tags

Design is a muted teal research-profile system: serif display headings on a system sans body, sticky nav with active state, card grids, tag chips. Responsive, with a print stylesheet, `prefers-reduced-motion` support, skip link, and ARIA landmarks. No third-party fonts, analytics or scripts — the pages make no request to any origin but their own.

## 3. Verification

`npm run build` and `npm run check` both pass. The checker gates the deploy on internal links, escaping, required content and nav presence. I negative-tested it: injecting a root-relative link into a post page produced `broken link … resolves to posts/blog.html` and exit 1.

That test caught a real bug — post pages sit one directory deeper, so every nav and asset link 404'd. `asset()` now takes a per-page depth. I also verified path-traversal handling (`/../../etc/passwd` and its percent-encoded form both return the 404 page, not `/etc/passwd`).

One caveat: the sandbox blocks network access including loopback, so I could not make a live HTTP request against `scripts/serve.js`. I verified its routing and traversal logic by replicating the path-resolution code in-process instead. Run `npm run serve` yourself to confirm it over the wire.

## 4. Git commands

The repo is initialised on `main` with one commit (`888b1d4`), no remote configured. Substitute your real URL for the dummy one:

```sh
cd my_website

# Add the remote and push
git remote add origin https://github.com/your-username/your-repo.git
git push --set-upstream origin main

# Run the workflow again without making a new commit (Actions → Deploy Site → Run workflow)
gh workflow run "Deploy Site" --repo your-username/your-repo --ref main

# Inspect runs and logs
gh run list   --repo your-username/your-repo --workflow "Deploy Site" --limit 10
gh run watch  --repo your-username/your-repo <run-id>
```

If the repository already has history you want to keep, use `git pull --rebase origin main` before pushing rather than `--force`.

Two settings to change in the GitHub UI, which I could not do from here: **Settings → Pages → Build and deployment → Source = GitHub Actions** (the official deploy action requires it), and **Settings → Actions → General → Workflow permissions → Read repository contents**, so the read-only default is enforced server-side rather than relying on the YAML alone.

